@tbl-overview shows the size of the data and how many variables are numeric
or character. Each row is an estimate for one place and one group of people,
not one person.

## Data Quality

```{r}
#| label: tbl-missing
#| tbl-cap: "Variables with missing values"

missing_values <- heart_data |>
  summarise(across(everything(), ~ sum(is.na(.x)))) |>
  pivot_longer(
    everything(),
    names_to = "Variable",
    values_to = "Missing_Values"
  ) |>
  filter(Missing_Values > 0) |>
  arrange(desc(Missing_Values))

knitr::kable(missing_values)
```

@tbl-missing shows which variables have missing values. The death rate
(`Data_Value`) is missing in 43,726 of the 78,792 rows, which is about 55%.
The CDC footnote for all of these rows says "Insufficient Data." So more than
half of the place and group combinations have no rate, and this will make
some comparisons harder, especially for counties. The footnote columns are
only empty when a rate is there, and the coordinates are missing in just 24
rows. Before I start exploring, I will check which places and groups are
missing rates.

## Dataset Complexity

I think this dataset is complex enough because it has a lot of rows and
different kinds of variables: places, groups, and numbers. County, state, and
national rows are mixed together, so I will need to filter and group with
tidyverse functions to keep them apart. The rate column also has to be
changed to numbers before I can use it. To compare parts of the country, I
plan to make a region variable with `case_when()`. I would like feedback on
whether this is enough for the project.

## Planned Explorations

1. How do death rates differ across states and counties?
2. Are rates different for men and women?
3. How do rates compare across racial and ethnic groups?
4. Which regions have the highest and lowest rates?

